{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Both addressed. `main` at `92111d4`.\n\n## 1. You're right, and it's worse than I said\n\nFirst, a correction to the mechanism: the old notes aren't \"attached to the old commits.\" A note's content lives in the **notes ref's own commit chain**, not on the commit it annotates. So the question is only ever whether that chain still reaches the old blob.\n\nNow the two cases, both measured just now in a scratch repo:\n\n**Already pushed** → the squash is skipped entirely (the code checks `ls-remote` first and warns). Old blob stays in the ref's ancestry. Redaction = \"removed from the current view,\" nothing more. The tool does say this.\n\n**Never pushed** → the squash runs, `cledger redact` prints `history squashed: yes`… **and the secret is still recoverable.** I pulled it straight back out:\n\n```\ngit show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print $1}')\n→ FOUND in reflog entry 8bdb47b\n```\n\n`update-ref` leaves reflog entries pointing at the discarded commits. It stays recoverable for the reflog expiry window — 90 days by default — and only actually vanishes after `git reflog expire --expire=now` plus a pruning `gc`, which I confirmed does clear it.\n\nSo yes: **redaction is not currently erasure, even in the case the tool reports as full success.** Your instinct found a real gap, and the misleading part is the output claiming `history squashed: yes`.\n\n**Where I'd still push back on history-walking reads** — but for a different reason than I gave. You're right that it doesn't *create* the exposure; the blob is already there. The problem is it *promotes* it from \"recoverable by deliberate digging\" to \"printed by every read.\" And concretely: `dedupById` breaks ties by earliest `recorded_at`, so a history-walking read would actively **prefer the un-redacted copy** over the redacted one. That's precisely backwards. It'd also break `git notes show` interop and make reads O(entire notes history).\n\nSo the fix belongs on the redaction side — make it actually redact — not on the read side.\n\n**I need one decision from you before implementing that.** Expiring the ledger ref's reflog is narrow and safe. But *reclaiming* the objects needs a prune, and prune is repo-wide: it would also drop unreachable objects you might want, like dropped stashes or abandoned commits. My recommendation is the middle path — run the scoped `git reflog expire --expire=now refs/notes/conversation-ledger` (safe, makes the objects prunable, deletes nothing else), stop printing `history squashed: yes`, and say plainly that the objects remain until the next `gc`, with a `--prune` flag for people who want it now. Want that, or would you rather it just prune outright?\n\nNoted on post-push purge being impossible — that matches what the docs already say: rotating the credential is the only real remedy once it's shared.\n\n## 2. What point 2 was\n\nShort version: in 0.14.0 I made the ledger push **branch-scoped**, so pushing a branch only shares that branch's conversations instead of every branch's. But the pre-push hook couldn't see *which* branch you were pushing, so it just used whatever was checked out. That meant `git push origin feat` while sitting on `main` shared nothing of feat — which you said was weird, and it was.\n\nFixed in 0.15.0: the hook now passes git's pre-push input through, so the scope follows the refs you're actually pushing. It also needed a second change — the installer used to skip any hook file that already had cledger's marker, so the fix would only ever have reached brand-new repos; it now upgrades the marked block in place, which I verified happened live on this repo.\n\n## Heads up again\n\n`src/redact/scan.ts` and `src/store.ts` have uncommitted **code** changes in your main checkout from another session — different files from mine, so the merge went through cleanly, but they're still sitting there uncommitted. `scripts/inspect-finding.py` is also still untracked.\n\nneeds input: whether `cledger redact` should expire the notes reflog only (safe, objects linger until the next gc) or also prune outright (repo-wide, drops other unreachable objects) — I'd take the former plus honest output and an opt-in `--prune`.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"f6c0bab7a93d68dd6aff2cb6b3ba0ce3d79f3e89","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","seq":1452},"id":"ev1-e94626118071b2fa5cb450c1e8ecaaeefceb9eaee37b5f4e3ef8e028665d67cb","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-07-30T14:34:28.833Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"cef69ec2-8b5a-4e91-8078-ac955e039295","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.220","tool":"cledger","version":"0.16.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"Both addressed. `main` at `92111d4`.\n\n## 1. You're right, and it's worse than I said\n\nFirst, a correction to the mechanism: the old notes aren't \"attached to the old commits.\" A note's content lives in the **notes ref's own commit chain**, not on the commit it annotates. So the question is only ever whether that chain still reaches the old blob.\n\nNow the two cases, both measured just now in a scratch repo:\n\n**Already pushed** → the squash is skipped entirely (the code checks `ls-remote` first and warns). Old blob stays in the ref's ancestry. Redaction = \"removed from the current view,\" nothing more. The tool does say this.\n\n**Never pushed** → the squash runs, `cledger redact` prints `history squashed: yes`… **and the secret is still recoverable.** I pulled it straight back out:\n\n```\ngit show $(git reflog show refs/notes/conversation-ledger | awk '{print